      "text": "What makes things in here unique ?",
      "fontSize": 24.287500000000005,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What makes things in here unique ?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5gWyx74VfFbdkgTXjm_so",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 651.62109375,
      "y": 690.77734375,
      "width": 450.1015625,
      "height": 2425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aN",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1818342121,
      "version": 3640,
      "versionNonce": 339539977,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779672472099,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "It seems as if everyone it utilizing these\nbuilding blocks. In different ways maybe. \n1. Tools\n    - Bash commands\n    - Communication (bots, email, teams, slack)\n    - file generation\n    - visualizations\n    - statistical tools\n    - querying\n    - anything that existed as a feature of a\nsoftware , can now be added as a tool to an\nagent.\n    - all you have to do is make a good doc\nstring and a good set of instructions.\n    - rag - vector embeddings of text docs\nfor retrieval and info extraction. \n    - mcp servers (every software offers one)\n2. System Prompts \n    - These are the prioritized. Guardrails ,\npersonalities, hard stops, capabilities.\n    - There is not much here.\n    - Role, task, context, constraints and so\non. \"Prompt engineering\"\n    - each model will behave differently to the\nsame system prompt.\n3. Instructions\n    - How to use tools\n    - how to format\n    - how to output results\n    - what not to do\n    - LLMs can generate these for you\n4. Sub-Agents\n    - sub agents are valuable\n    - we can spin up 5 agents in parallel, have\nthem all do deep research on a topic, and\nhave a summarizer agent analyze the results\nand find the most common response, or\ndivergence.\n    - is it like a ensemble method / random\nforest / K-folds. We are taking the average\nresponse.\n    - we can also have agents do work in\nparallel, each can handle a task.\n    - each agent can have a different task.\n    - This is really important for ANI,\nartificial narrow intelligence. \n    - How this is being done today is very\nuseful.\n    - currently using ASYNC / what about if\nwe used parallel processing ? I am not sure\nthat would apply here.\n5. Context\n    - any additional context to the agent\n    - sometimes too much is harmful\n    - how do we find the just right amount of\ncontext ?\n6. Memory\n    - short term memory / long term memory\n    - short term , tracking across sessions.\nKeeps up with the conversation. Things\ntalked about in the past.\n    - long term memory is user preferences,\nthings they like, their name etc.\n    - Long term memory is sometimes helpful\nhowever when I am in a totally different\ntopic and my agent is referencing a project\nfrom a month ago , it is not helpful\n    - ? How does long term memory work most\nplaces today\n    - ? How can I implement it and make it\nbetter\n7. Skills\n    - just context bundles\n    - currently using obsidian as my brain\n    - .md files describing certain ways I like to\ndo things\n    - helps the agent understand best\npractices on an array of topics.\n    - 'building an agent', 'creating a ppt'\n    - these are great\n    - great way to make instructions smaller\nand cheaper per turn.\n    - use cosine similarity off of the\ndescription.\n8. Evals\n     - LLm as a judge rubric based\n     - deterministic\n        - contains\n        - spans\n\nHow do we get outside of this box and add\nsomething truly unique to a project? LLM fine\ntuning ? Applying it to the real world? Doing\nsomething that actually benefits humanity ->\nfinancial literacy , education , mapping out\nwater wells for impoverished countries. \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "It seems as if everyone it utilizing these building blocks. In different ways maybe. \n1. Tools\n    - Bash commands\n    - Communication (bots, email, teams, slack)\n    - file generation\n    - visualizations\n    - statistical tools\n    - querying\n    - anything that existed as a feature of a software , can now be added as a tool to an agent.\n    - all you have to do is make a good doc string and a good set of instructions.\n    - rag - vector embeddings of text docs for retrieval and info extraction. \n    - mcp servers (every software offers one)\n2. System Prompts \n    - These are the prioritized. Guardrails , personalities, hard stops, capabilities.\n    - There is not much here.\n    - Role, task, context, constraints and so on. \"Prompt engineering\"\n    - each model will behave differently to the same system prompt.\n3. Instructions\n    - How to use tools\n    - how to format\n    - how to output results\n    - what not to do\n    - LLMs can generate these for you\n4. Sub-Agents\n    - sub agents are valuable\n    - we can spin up 5 agents in parallel, have them all do deep research on a topic, and have a summarizer agent analyze the results and find the most common response, or divergence.\n    - is it like a ensemble method / random forest / K-folds. We are taking the average response.\n    - we can also have agents do work in parallel, each can handle a task.\n    - each agent can have a different task.\n    - This is really important for ANI, artificial narrow intelligence. \n    - How this is being done today is very useful.\n    - currently using ASYNC / what about if we used parallel processing ? I am not sure that would apply here.\n5. Context\n    - any additional context to the agent\n    - sometimes too much is harmful\n    - how do we find the just right amount of context ?\n6. Memory\n    - short term memory / long term memory\n    - short term , tracking across sessions. Keeps up with the conversation. Things talked about in the past.\n    - long term memory is user preferences, things they like, their name etc.\n    - Long term memory is sometimes helpful however when I am in a totally different topic and my agent is referencing a project from a month ago , it is not helpful\n    - ? How does long term memory work most places today\n    - ? How can I implement it and make it better\n7. Skills\n    - just context bundles\n    - currently using obsidian as my brain\n    - .md files describing certain ways I like to do things\n    - helps the agent understand best practices on an array of topics.\n    - 'building an agent', 'creating a ppt'\n    - these are great\n    - great way to make instructions smaller and cheaper per turn.\n    - use cosine similarity off of the description.\n8. Evals\n     - LLm as a judge rubric based\n     - deterministic\n        - contains\n        - spans\n\nHow do we get outside of this box and add something truly unique to a project? LLM fine tuning ? Applying it to the real world? Doing something that actually benefits humanity -> financial literacy , education , mapping out water wells for impoverished countries. \n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
